# Callback Functions

We react to events by handing `addEventListener` a **callback function**. A callback function is a function passed into another function as an argument, to be called (or "called back") at a later time. This lesson is about those functions themselves: why we pass them the way we do, and the several ways JavaScript lets us write them.

## Why No Parentheses

Here is the pattern we have been writing:

```html id=page1
<button id="target">Click me</button>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const el = document.querySelector('#target');

function callback() {
  console.log('reacted to the click');
}

el.addEventListener('click', callback);
```
```+id=page1
```

The part worth explaining is why `callback` has no parentheses after it.

Recall from [Course 1](<../../Course 1/Module 3 - Introduction to Programming with JavaScript/21 - Invoking functions.webnb#referencing-versus-calling>) that `callback` and `callback()` are different expressions:

- `callback` is the **function object itself**.
- `callback()` **calls** the function, and the value of that expression is whatever the function returned.

`addEventListener` needs a function object, because it is going to hold onto it and call it later. If we write `callback()`, the function runs immediately, and what gets handed to `addEventListener` is its return value. Our callback has no `return` statement, so that value is `undefined`, and we end up registering `undefined` as the listener. Nothing is listening.

Watch it fail:

```{javascript autorun}
const el = document.querySelector('#target');

function callback() {
  console.log('reacted to the click');
}

el.addEventListener('click', callback());
```
```+id=page1
```

The message appears the moment the cell runs, before you touch anything, and then clicking the button does nothing at all. Those two symptoms together always mean a stray pair of parentheses.

## Four Ways to Write a Function

We have been declaring functions one way. There are several, and all four of these define the same `add` function.

**A traditional function declaration**, with a name:

```{javascript autorun}
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

console.log(add(2, 3));
```

**An anonymous function**, which is the same thing with the name left off. A function with no name is called **anonymous**. On its own an anonymous function has to be used immediately or stored somewhere, so here we give it a name by assigning it to a variable:

```{javascript autorun}
const add = function (a, b) {
  return a + b;
};

console.log(add(2, 3));
```

**An arrow function**, which drops the `function` keyword entirely and puts `=>` between the parameters and the body:

```{javascript autorun}
const add = (a, b) => {
  return a + b;
};

console.log(add(2, 3));
```

**An arrow function with an implicit return.** When the whole body is a single expression, we can leave off the curly braces and the `return`. The value of that expression is returned automatically:

```{javascript autorun}
const add = (a, b) => a + b;

console.log(add(2, 3));
```

All four take two numbers and give back their sum. In this specialization we will mostly use the first form, a named function declaration, and the anonymous arrow function.

## Callbacks Written Inline

Anonymous functions matter for callbacks because a callback often does not need a name. It is used in exactly one place, as the second argument to `addEventListener`, so naming it can be more ceremony than it is worth.

Compare these three. They behave identically:

```html id=page2
<button id="named">Named</button>
<button id="expression">Anonymous</button>
<button id="arrow">Arrow</button>
<p id="log">Click a button.</p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const named = document.querySelector('#named');
const expression = document.querySelector('#expression');
const arrow = document.querySelector('#arrow');
const log = document.querySelector('#log');

function showNamed() {
  log.textContent = 'named function callback';
}

named.addEventListener('click', showNamed);

expression.addEventListener('click', function () {
  log.textContent = 'anonymous function callback';
});

arrow.addEventListener('click', () => {
  log.textContent = 'arrow function callback';
});
```
```+id=page2
```

The last one, an anonymous arrow function written directly inside the call, is what you will see most often in real code and in the rest of this course.

Use a named function when the callback is long, when you want to reuse it, or when you will need to remove the listener later, which is the subject of a lesson coming up shortly. Use an inline arrow function when it is a couple of lines that belong to this one listener.

## Exercises

1. Write `double` as an arrow function with an implicit return, so that `double(5)` gives `10`.

    ```{javascript}
    // Write your code here

    console.log(double(5));
    ```
    ```+solution
    const double = (n) => n * 2;

    console.log(double(5));
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(double(5)).should('equal', 10).run('`double(5)` should return `10`', 'double works');
    wrap(double(0)).should('equal', 0).run('`double(0)` should return `0`', 'double works for zero');
    wrap(source.includes('=>')).should('equal', true).run('Write it as an arrow function, using `=>`', 'Used arrow syntax');
    wrap(source.includes('return')).should('equal', false).run('Use an implicit return, with no curly braces and no `return` keyword', 'Used an implicit return');
    ```

2. Rewrite this listener to use an anonymous arrow function written directly inside the `addEventListener` call, with no separate named function.

    ```html id=page3
    <button id="go">Go</button>
    <p id="out">Waiting</p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const button = document.querySelector('#go');
    const out = document.querySelector('#out');

    function handleClick() {
      out.textContent = 'Done';
    }

    button.addEventListener('click', handleClick);
    ```
    ```+id=page3
    ```
    ```+solution
    const button = document.querySelector('#go');
    const out = document.querySelector('#out');

    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      out.textContent = 'Done';
    });
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#go').click().run('Could not find the `#go` button', 'Clicked the button');
    assert('#out').should('have.text', 'Done').run('Clicking should still set the paragraph to `Done`', 'The listener still works');
    wrap(source.includes('=>')).should('equal', true).run('Use an arrow function', 'Used arrow syntax');
    wrap(source.includes('function handleClick')).should('equal', false).run('Remove the separate named function and write the callback inline', 'Removed the named function');
    ```

3. This listener never fires, and its message appears as soon as the cell runs. Fix it.

    ```html id=page4
    <button id="fixme">Demo</button>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const button = document.querySelector('#fixme');

    function handleClick() {
      console.log('Handled');
    }

    button.addEventListener('click', handleClick());
    ```
    ```+id=page4
    ```
    ```+solution
    const button = document.querySelector('#fixme');

    function handleClick() {
      console.log('Handled');
    }

    button.addEventListener('click', handleClick);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(console.history().length).should('equal', 0).run('Nothing should be logged until the button is clicked. Remove the parentheses so the function is passed rather than called', 'The callback waits for the click');
    assert('#fixme').click().run('Could not find the `#fixme` button', 'Clicked the button');
    check.consoleLogged('Handled').run('Clicking the button should log `Handled`', 'The listener fires on click');
    ```

